    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        470,
        700
      ],
      "size": [
        470,
        300
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 16,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "MOTION CONCEPT REFMOD \u2014 can a video refmod carry MOVEMENT?\n\nTHE HYPOTHESIS, AND WHY IT IS NOT OBVIOUS\n  We have it recorded that motion and appearance are the SAME TOKENS on a\n  reference video: no setting separates them, and a reference video drags its\n  subject's look in with its movement. A refmod is a different object. Pooling\n  is SPATIAL only -- `--pool` sets the latent h/w while `--latent-frames` keeps\n  time -- so a heavily pooled multi-frame latent is mostly-time and\n  almost-no-appearance. Whether H3 reads that as motion is genuinely unknown.\n\n  If it does, a motion is a ~1K-token concept you can drop on any render. If it\n  does not, we have closed a real question cheaply.\n\nTHE PROMPT IS THE EXPERIMENT\n  It gives the subject, the room and the framing and says NOTHING about what she\n  does or how the camera moves. Any motion that appears is the refmod's. It does\n  not ask for stillness either -- prompts name the failure they cause, and\n  \"stands still\" would suppress exactly what is being measured.\n\n  Full-body wide, locked-off camera, no dialogue. You cannot judge movement in a\n  close-up, and dialogue drives head and mouth motion that would confound it.\n\nMAKING THE MOTION REFMOD\n  From C:\\SD\\H3_RefMod_Studio_v1_2\\h3_refmod_vendor, with ComfyUI's own\n  Python (Studio's GUI is encode-only and cannot do this):\n\n    python extract_mod.py --mode training --pool 8 --latent-frames 16\n        --identity 0 --concept-type pose_motion\n        --video a.mp4 --video b.mp4 --video c.mp4 ...\n        --vae <h3 video vae> --name walk_concept\n\n  Settings, and why they differ from the body-type recipe in H3 45:\n\n    --pool 8         HARDER than the 16 used for body type. The whole\n                     hypothesis is that destroying appearance leaves motion, so\n                     start where appearance cannot survive: 8x8 = 16 tokens per\n                     frame, 16 frames = 256 tokens total.\n    --latent-frames  the axis carrying the movement. 16 is the default and the\n                     thing NOT to economise on here.\n    --identity 0     refinement is MSE against the full latent. Whether it helps\n                     a motion concept is unknown -- the low-frequency structure\n                     it can represent may be exactly the movement, or it may pull\n                     appearance back. Start at 0, which is fast, and raise it as\n                     a SECOND experiment rather than a first guess.\n    --multiplier     exists so a short clip is not drowned out by the target's\n                     own tokens. Reach for it only if the concept reads as\n                     nothing at all.\n\n  Source clips: MANY DIFFERENT PEOPLE doing the SAME movement, varied bodies,\n  clothes, rooms and lighting. The mechanism is plain averaging, so what the\n  clips share survives and everything else cancels. Built from one dancer you\n  get that dancer, not the dance. Up to 60 frames are sampled uniformly per\n  clip.\n\nREADING THE RESULT \u2014 three outcomes, not two\n  1. NOTHING CHANGES. The concept is inert at this pool size. Raise to --pool\n     16, or --multiplier 2, before concluding anything.\n  2. THE MOVEMENT APPEARS. The win. Confirm on a second seed before believing\n     it -- one seed tells you something changed, not that it generalises.\n  3. APPEARANCE BLEEDS IN. The known failure: she stops looking like the\n     character, or the rehearsal room turns into a source clip's location. That\n     is identity/background surviving the pooling, and it means the dataset was\n     not diverse enough -- not that the idea is wrong.\n\n  Outcome 3 is why the character anchors stay wired: contamination is only\n  visible if you know what she is supposed to look like.\n\nHOW TO RUN IT\n  1. Seed is fixed at 2024. Leave it alone between arms.\n  2. Render once with the loader's refmod on `(none)` -- the control.\n  3. Select the motion refmod, render again. Change nothing else.\n  4. Then one variable at a time: pool size, then --identity, then the dataset.\n\n  `strength` is NOT a LoRA weight -- lower values blend the reference toward a\n  BLURRED latent. Leave it at 1 until the concept does something.\n\nWHY NOT IN 38\n  Single chunk, short, one variable. The long-form recipe works now, a 4th\n  reference has already wrecked motion once, and this one is AIMED at motion.\n  Keep it here until it is known-good."
      ],
      "title": "How to use"
    },
